`var` is function-scoped, so there is one `i` shared by all three callbacks. The `setTimeout` callbacks run only after the loop finishes, by which point `i` is 3 (the value that failed the `i < 3` test). Each callback reads that same variable and logs 3.

Fixes:

```js
for (let i = 0; i < 3; i++) {   // let creates a fresh binding per iteration
  setTimeout(() => console.log(i), 0);
}
```

Or, keeping `var`, capture the value per iteration with an IIFE or by passing it as an argument:

```js
for (var i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
  setTimeout((j) => console.log(j), 0, i);
}
```
